{
  "title": "How Freelance Designers Can Avoid Burnout",
  "seriesTag": "Designer Wellness",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Burnout Doesn't Announce Itself — Until It's Too Late",
      "body": "Most freelance designers don't see it coming. One day the work feels heavy, the ideas stop flowing, and even easy projects feel impossible. Here's how to stay ahead of it.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Set Working Hours and Actually Keep Them",
      "body": "Without a fixed schedule, client work bleeds into every hour. Define your start and end times, communicate them clearly, and treat them like a contract with yourself.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Price Your Work to Protect Your Energy",
      "body": "Chronic under-pricing forces you to take on too many projects just to survive. Raise your rates, reduce your client load, and give each project the attention it deserves.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Build a 'No' Muscle",
      "body": "Not every project is worth your bandwidth. Saying no to poor-fit clients, rushed timelines, or scope creep isn't lost income — it's preserved capacity for better work.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Schedule Recovery Time Like It's a Deliverable",
      "body": "Breaks, weekends off, and vacations aren't luxuries — they're maintenance. Block them in your calendar before clients fill that space for you.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Create a Separation Ritual Between Work and Rest",
      "body": "A short walk, closing your laptop, or changing rooms signals to your brain that the workday is over. Rituals create the boundary your home office can't.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Track Warning Signs Before They Become a Crisis",
      "body": "Irritability, missed deadlines, creative blocks, and dreading projects you once loved are early signals. Check in with yourself weekly and adjust before burnout sets in.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Protect Your Practice — Start With One Change This Week",
      "body": "Pick one boundary, one habit, or one policy from this list and put it in place today. Your long-term creative career depends on the decisions you make right now.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Cash-Flow Forecasting for First-Time Agency Owners",
  "seriesTag": "Agency Finance 101",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Your agency looks profitable. So why is your bank account empty?",
      "body": "Revenue and cash are not the same thing. Most first-time agency owners learn this the hard way. Here's how to stop the panic and start forecasting.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Cash-flow forecasting is not accounting. It's a survival tool.",
      "body": "Accounting tells you what happened. Forecasting tells you what's coming. You need to know — 8 weeks from now — whether you can make payroll, pay contractors, and keep the lights on.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Start with your 'cash-in' calendar, not your invoices.",
      "body": "List every client. Note when they actually pay — not when you invoice. Net-30 clients who pay on day 45 are a cash-flow problem hiding in plain sight. Date of deposit is what matters.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Map your fixed cash-out dates with the same precision.",
      "body": "Payroll, software subscriptions, rent, contractor minimums — list them by the exact date they leave your account. You're building a 60-day picture of cash in versus cash out, week by week.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 'danger gap' is the week your out-dates beat your in-dates.",
      "body": "Every agency has one. It usually falls at the end of a month before retainer payments clear. Name your danger gap. Know its size. That number is what your operating reserve must cover.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "A simple forecast beats a complex spreadsheet you never open.",
      "body": "Use a single tab: dates down the left, projected in, projected out, and running balance. Update it every Friday. Fifteen minutes a week will catch problems 30 days before they become crises.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Three levers when your forecast turns red: speed, delay, or bridge.",
      "body": "Speed up collections — send invoices earlier, offer 2% early-pay discounts. Delay non-critical spend. Or bridge the gap with a line of credit you set up before you need it, not after.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Build your forecast this weekend. Your future self will thank you.",
      "body": "Download a free agency cash-flow template in the link below. Takes 90 minutes to set up, saves you from scrambling for cash at the worst possible time. Follow for more no-fluff agency finance.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Motivation Is A Lie (Here's What Actually Works)",
  "seriesTag": "Habit Myths Debunked",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Waiting For Motivation? That's Why Your Habits Keep Failing.",
      "body": "Everyone talks about motivation like it's the secret ingredient. It isn't. Here's the truth no one wants to say out loud.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #1: ",
      "body": "If you had to feel ready before starting, you'd never start. Motivation follows action — it almost never leads it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #2: ",
      "body": "Motivation is a feeling. Feelings are temporary. Habits built on feelings collapse the moment life gets hard.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Myth #3: ",
      "body": "Discipline isn't about willpower either. It's about removing the decision entirely. Systems beat self-control every time.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The Real Driver: Environment Design",
      "body": "Consistent people don't rely on feeling inspired. They engineer their surroundings so the habit is the path of least resistance.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 2-Minute Rule Changes Everything",
      "body": "If a habit takes less than 2 minutes to start, motivation becomes irrelevant. Start embarrassingly small. Show up. Build from there.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "What To Do Instead Of Waiting",
      "body": "1. Shrink the habit until it's effortless.\n2. Attach it to something you already do.\n3. Design your space to make it obvious.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Stop Chasing Motivation. Start Building Structure.",
      "body": "Save this post and audit one habit this week. What friction can you remove? Your future consistency depends on today's setup — not tomorrow's inspiration.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning",
  "seriesTag": "Strategy",
  "brand": {
    "apply": true,
    "paper": "#000000",
    "ink": "#FFFFFF",
    "accent": "#FF0000",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Why Most Products Fail Before Launch",
      "body": "It's rarely the product. It's almost always how it's positioned in the market.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "What Is Product Positioning?",
      "body": "Positioning defines who your product is for, what it does, and why it beats every alternative — in one clear idea.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 3 Questions That Shape Your Position",
      "body": "Who is this for? What problem does it solve? Why should they believe you over anyone else?",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Your Category Is a Choice",
      "body": "You decide which shelf your product sits on. Pick the wrong category and you fight the wrong competitors.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Differentiation vs. Distinction",
      "body": "Differentiation is functional. Distinction is emotional. The strongest positions deliver both at once.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test Before You Commit",
      "body": "Put your position in front of real prospects. If they don't get it in five seconds, rewrite it — not the product.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Ready to Own Your Position?",
      "body": "Map your market, define your edge, and build messaging that lands. Start with a single positioning statement this week.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning",
  "seriesTag": "Strategy Series",
  "brand": {
    "apply": true,
    "paper": "#000000",
    "ink": "#FFFFFF",
    "accent": "#FF0000",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Nobody Buys the Best Product",
      "body": "They buy the one they understand fastest. Positioning is the reason customers choose you — before they even try you.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "What Is Product Positioning?",
      "body": "It's the mental space your product occupies in your customer's mind. Not what you say about it — what they believe about it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The 5 Elements of Strong Positioning",
      "body": "1. Who it's for\n2. The category you compete in\n3. The problem you solve\n4. Your key differentiator\n5. Proof it works",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Most Brands Skip Step One",
      "body": "Trying to appeal to everyone means owning no one's attention. Narrow your audience to sharpen your message.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Category Design Changes Everything",
      "body": "Don't just win in an existing category — define a new one. The brand that names the category usually owns it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Test Your Positioning in One Sentence",
      "body": "Can a stranger repeat back what you do and who it's for after hearing it once? If not, keep refining.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Positioning Is Never 'Done'",
      "body": "Markets shift. Competitors move. Customer language evolves. Revisit your positioning every 6 months minimum.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Ready to Own Your Category?",
      "body": "Audit your current messaging against these five elements. Start with who — everything else follows. Share this with your team today.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Product Positioning for Early-Stage Founders",
  "seriesTag": "Founder Fundamentals",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Nobody buys your product. They buy a better version of themselves.",
      "body": "If your positioning starts with features, you've already lost. Here's how early-stage founders get it right.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Positioning is not your tagline.",
      "body": "It's the internal decision your customer makes about where you fit in their world. Your tagline is just the surface. Positioning is the foundation beneath it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Start with the problem, not the product.",
      "body": "Founders fall in love with what they built. Customers only care about what's broken in their life. Lead with the pain, then introduce the fix.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Who is it actually for?",
      "body": "Trying to appeal to everyone is a positioning failure. The sharper your ideal customer profile, the stronger your positioning signal. Niche down until it feels uncomfortable.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Name your category — or get trapped in someone else's.",
      "body": "If you don't define the category you belong in, buyers will slot you next to a competitor and judge you on their terms. Own the frame before the market does.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The positioning stress test.",
      "body": "Can your best customer explain what you do, who it's for, and why it matters — in one sentence? If not, your positioning needs another pass.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Reposition early. Repositioning late is expensive.",
      "body": "Changing how the market perceives you after you've scaled costs time, money, and trust. Get it right at the seed stage, when iteration is still cheap.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Your next move: pressure-test your positioning today.",
      "body": "Write down your one-sentence position statement. Share it with three customers who didn't come through a referral. Their confusion is your roadmap. Start there.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Weak Signal or Real Evidence? A PM's Field Test",
  "seriesTag": "Signal vs. Evidence",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "One customer complaint isn't a roadmap item.",
      "body": "Experienced PMs know the danger: a loud voice in a user interview shapes the next sprint. Here's how to tell the difference between noise and a pattern worth acting on.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "What makes a signal weak?",
      "body": "A signal is weak when it comes from one source, surfaces under leading questions, or disappears when you change the framing. Enthusiasm in a demo is not validation.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The repetition test",
      "body": "Has the problem appeared unprompted across at least five separate conversations — different segments, different contexts? If you had to fish for it, it doesn't count.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The specificity test",
      "body": "Vague pain is cheap. Ask: can the customer describe the last time this cost them time, money, or a decision? Concrete recall is a stronger indicator than general frustration.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The behavior test",
      "body": "What are people doing right now to work around the problem? Workarounds — spreadsheets, Slack bots, manual exports — are evidence. 'We'd probably use that' is not.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The consistency test",
      "body": "Run the same core question through three different formats: interview, support ticket review, and usage data. If only one format surfaces the problem, treat it as a hypothesis, not a finding.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "This week: audit your top three backlog items",
      "body": "Pick three items sitting near the top of your roadmap. Write down which test each one passes. If an item fails two or more, move it to a discovery column before it blocks real work.",
      "isCover": false
    }
  ]
}
